# Line-ending and diff policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS LOAD-BEARING, not boilerplate:
# Git decides "text vs binary" by scanning the first 8000 bytes for a NUL. A minimal,
# uncompressed, hand-written PDF -- exactly what corpus/malformed/ consists of -- is pure
# ASCII and gets misdetected as text. With core.autocrlf=true (the default on this build
# machine) checkout then injects a CR before every LF, and since PDF's xref table stores
# ABSOLUTE BYTE OFFSETS, every offset past the first newline becomes wrong. The file is
# structurally broken, or worse, silently becomes a DIFFERENT malformed file than the test
# was written against.
#
# This is also what keeps tests/roundtrip honest: it asserts untouched objects are
# byte-identical, so a Windows checkout and the Linux portable-asan checkout must produce
# identical bytes from the same commit. eol=lf below is what guarantees that.
#
# .gitattributes is NOT retroactive. Rules must exist before the bytes land.

# Default: normalize in the repository, check out LF everywhere including Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Never touch these. Test data and reference images -- byte-exact by definition.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
corpus/**   binary
golden/**   binary

# `diff` IS SPELLED OUT ON EVERY LINE BELOW, AND ITS ABSENCE IS WHY THIS BLOCK EXISTS TWICE.
# `binary` is a MACRO for `-text -diff -merge`, so pulling a file back out of it takes all three
# words. T4's fix supplied `text` and `merge` and not `diff` -- which restored correct line endings
# and stopped `git` from refusing to merge, and left `git diff` still printing `Bin 23443 -> 24421
# bytes` for corpus/PROVENANCE.md and corpus/*.json. That is exactly the outcome the note below says
# it fixed, for two of the three file types it names; only `corpus/*.py` escaped, because
# `diff=python` sets the attribute as a side effect of choosing a driver. Found at Stage 2 T17, when
# golden/manifest.json was committed with the same two words and landed as `Bin` in the same way
# (issue **I-16** in Stage2-Rendering-Testing-Guide.md). The attribute is applied when a diff is
# RENDERED, not when a blob is stored, so adding the word here fixes every past commit as well.

# ...but corpus/ is no longer only data. Stage 2 T4 put the GENERATORS in there too, and
# `corpus/** binary` marks them binary as well -- so `git diff` shows nothing for several
# thousand lines of Python and Markdown, and a change to a generator cannot be reviewed. The
# last matching pattern wins per attribute, so these three lines pull the source files back
# out; the .pdf data below stays binary, which is the part that actually matters (a PDF xref
# stores absolute byte offsets, and CRLF injection shifts every one of them).
corpus/*.py     text eol=lf diff=python merge
corpus/*.md     text eol=lf diff merge
corpus/*.json   text eol=lf diff merge
# Stage 5 T1's sidecars, one directory down, where `corpus/*.json` does not reach (a pattern with a
# slash is anchored, and `*` does not cross one). They are ground truth a reviewer must be able to
# read in a diff -- a label edited to match a wrong answer is the change this project most needs to
# see -- and `eol=lf` keeps them byte-identical on checkout, which `--verify` requires.
corpus/labels/*.json    text eol=lf diff merge
corpus/compare/*.json   text eol=lf diff merge

# ...and golden/ is no longer only data either, for exactly the same reason and with the same fix.
# Stage 2 T17 put manifest.json (which cases are baselined, hand-authored), captured.json (what the
# capture measured) and two Markdown records in there, and `golden/** binary` would mark all four
# binary -- so the file that says WHY a baseline moved would be the one file a reviewer cannot read
# in a diff. That is precisely the defect T4 found here and fixed three lines above; finding it
# again in the next directory is what a rule written once and applied twice looks like. The .png
# baselines stay binary, which is the half that matters.
golden/*.json   text eol=lf diff merge
golden/*.md     text eol=lf diff merge

*.pdf   binary
*.fdf   binary
*.xfdf  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.tif   binary
*.tiff  binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary

# Compiled Qt translation catalogs (the .ts sources are text, the .qm outputs are not)
*.qm    binary

# Prebuilt binaries and archives (the pinned PDFium DLL and its import library)
*.dll   binary
*.dll.a binary
*.lib   binary
*.a     binary
*.exe   binary
*.pdb   binary
*.zip   binary
*.7z    binary
*.tgz   binary
*.gz    binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files whose interpreter or host genuinely wants CRLF on Windows
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.psm1  text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.sln   text eol=crlf
*.rc    text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Diff and merge behaviour
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.h     diff=cpp
*.hpp   diff=cpp
*.cpp   diff=cpp
*.cc    diff=cpp

# Qt translation catalogs and generated license reports: never auto-merge, the
# resolution is always "regenerate", never "splice two versions together".
*.ts           text merge=union
THIRD-PARTY.md text merge=ours

# Keep design docs and test data out of GitHub's language statistics
claude-plan/** linguist-documentation
corpus/**      linguist-vendored
golden/**      linguist-vendored
